perhaps the best way to measure the  impact of a pandemic is to look at how  many more people are dying there might  usually be expected at this time of year  what we call excess deaths these include  people not officially diagnosed with  Kover 19:00 and those who died  indirectly for example have a heart  attack but don't go to A&amp;E now Sky News  has analyzed the latest data on European  country since the start of the outbreak  on the 2nd of March to the 12th of April  from Euro Momo the network that collects  it it uses an algorithm to compare  countries and the gray area here shows  the average level in a normal year the  UK has split into its constituent  nations the blue line flashing here is  England and as you can see along with  Italy and Spain England has the highest  levels of excess deaths so far 18 points  above the overall average it reached  this in the week ending the 3rd of April  the week after Italy and Spain but as  you can see England was the only country  to continue that trend into the  following week the week it's thought the  UK peaked in fact if we just look at the  UK's Four Nations we also see that  England's excess deaths in the last few  weeks have been far higher than those in  Scotland Wales and Northern Ireland up  here in total since Europe's outbreak  began around 57 thousand more people  than expected have died in the 24  countries studied that's an increase of  18 percent almost 53,000 of those deaths  are among people aged over 65 which is  20% higher than usual but even younger  age groups those aged between 15 and 64  recorded more deaths than normal around  6000 in total up almost 14 percent with  countries recording their covert 19  deaths differently this data is really  valuable it gives us a clearer picture  than we've had yet of how many extra  deaths the virus could be causing
 

